==================================================================================================
REVISED FEASIBILITY: WHICH CLUSTERS STILL DECIDE.  The joint profile dims the outer bins by the envelope ratio (0.23-0.40x at 2 R500, median 0.30x), so the per-cluster decision power shifts to the bright end.  Planck 143 GHz, 23-31' bin (the 2 R500 bin): joint SNR > 3 in 2/12 clusters -- A2319, RXC1825 (SNR A2319:13.7, RXC1825:3.6); A644's joint prediction itself sits in the 'neither' branch (its own band [-6.89,-6.09], 23-31' joint SNR 0.1) and A1644 (y0 = 1.9e-5, the weakest amplitude) drops to 2.9 -- both contribute the pooled median and the full-curve chi2, not their own outer bins.  The 8-17' phantom-zone bins survive on ALL 12 (median joint SNR 12.2/6.9, min 7.8/3.8), the ACT/SO branch holds every bin > 3 sigma on the 7 in-band clusters (median 23-49), and the sample-level statements are intact: pooled slope error 0.238 (2-bin; 0.152 multi-bin; phantom 2-bin 0.093) -> the F2' (dust-kill) axis separates at 4 sigma pooled, the F1' (steeper) axis at 2 (2-bin) / 3 (multi-bin) sigma pooled.  The two-sector test on the outer bins separates joint vs phantom at 48 sigma shape-only (37 absolute).  The decision remains sample-level and bright-cluster-led -- as G129 already registered -- and the dimming does NOT remove any cluster from the pooled statements.

THE AMENDMENT'S DELTA: the tSZ test is upgraded from a 1-SECTOR to a 2-SECTOR discriminator with a 3-WAY decision tree.  G129's proposal could only say pass/kill against the phantom-only -1.44 curve: a slope in (-1.7, -0.9) passed, steeper than -2 killed the G095 extension, flatter killed the phantom reading -- one hypothesis under test, and the dust envelope was invisible to it (its effect was, unbeknownst to the 1-sector design, already IN the pass window's physics: G141 shows the phantom-alone reading is not what the framework actually predicts once the closed coherency is included).  The amended proposal tests BOTH sectors at once: the joint curve (G113 x G122, zero new parameters) is the prediction, the falsifiers are F1' (steeper than the joint band -> dust envelope killed) and F2' (at the phantom-only -1.44 -> dust killed, phantom stands), and the measurement returns THREE verdicts -- joint reading (-2.7..-2.05), phantom-only (-1.8..-1.2), or neither (steeper than -2.7), with the grey zones registered.  The cost of the upgrade is honestly stated: the joint prediction is DIMMER outside (0.30x at 2 R500), the outer bins lose signal, and the per-cluster slope error grows from 0.32 to 0.82 (2-bin; 0.53 multi-bin; pooled 0.093 -> 0.238 / 0.152); the 23-40' bins drop below 3 sigma in median on the archived Planck depth, the decision leans on the bright clusters, the pooled sample, the ACT/SO 8-18' overlap, and SO deep fields (which can still add the integration).  THE ASYMMETRY: the two falsifiers do NOT have equal power -- F2' (dust-kill, at the phantom-only -1.44) resolves at 4.4-6.9 sigma pooled, but F1' (the steeper-than-joint kill, the 'neither' branch) is only 2.6 sigma pooled (multi-bin): the measurement confidently separates JOINT from PHANTOM-ONLY, and can only POINT toward 'a different envelope slope', not kill for it.  Net: the same observation now discriminates THREE readings instead of testing ONE -- the amendment converts a pass/fail test into a classification whose strong leg is the dust-kill axis and whose weak leg (the steeper-than-joint branch) is a registered ~2.6-sigma pointer, exactly the honesty the upgrade demands.

